1
00:00:00.059 --> 00:00:04.320
Let's go into the types of knowledge.

2
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.220
The first type of knowledge is declarative knowledge.

3
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.730
And those are things like statements of fact.

4
00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:13.080
And this is where my email came into play.

5
00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.500
If you read it all the way to the bottom,

6
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.960
you would have entered a raffle.

7
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.890
So a statement of fact for today's lecture

8
00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.280
is, someone will win a prize before class ends.

9
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.820
And the prize was a Google Cardboard.

10
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.890
Google state-of-the-art virtual reality glasses.

11
00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:31.474
And I have them right here.

12
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.950
Yea.

13
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.650
I delivered on my promise.

14
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.770
That's a statement of fact.

15
00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.600
So pretend I'm a machine.

16
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.480
OK?

17
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.990
I don't know anything except what you tell me.

18
00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:48.100
I don't know.

19
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.320
I know that you tell me this statement.

20
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.847
I'm like, OK.

21
00:00:51.847 --> 00:00:53.930
But how is someone going to win a Google Cardboard

22
00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.459
before class ends, right?

23
00:00:55.459 --> 00:00:57.250
That's where imperative knowledge comes in.

24
00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.530
Imperative knowledge is the recipe, or the how-to,

25
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:02.230
or the sequence of steps.

26
00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.780
Sorry.

27
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.210
That's just my funny for that one.

28
00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:13.530
So the sequence of steps is imperative knowledge.

29
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.930
If I'm a machine, you need to tell me

30
00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.910
how someone will win a Google Cardboard before class.

31
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.680
If I follow these steps, then technically,

32
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.460
I should reach a conclusion.

33
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.950
Step one, I think we've already done that.

34
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.590
Whoever wanted to sign up has signed up.

35
00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.180
Now I'm going to open my IDE.

36
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.680
I'm just basically being a machine

37
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.740
and following the steps that you've told me.

38
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.631
The IDE that we're using in this class is called Anaconda.

39
00:01:41.631 --> 00:01:45.330
I'm just scrolling down to the bottom.

40
00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.660
Hopefully, you've installed it in problem set zero.

41
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.610
I've opened my IDE.

42
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.012
I'm going to follow the next set of instructions.

43
00:01:54.012 --> 00:01:56.220
I'm going to choose a random number between the first

44
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.460
and the nth responder.

45
00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.340
Now, I'm going to actually use Python to do this .

46
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.620
And this is also an example of how just

47
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.310
a really simple task in your life,

48
00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.742
you can use computers or programming to do that.

49
00:02:08.742 --> 00:02:10.200
Because if I chose a random number,

50
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.824
I might be biased because, for example,

51
00:02:11.824 --> 00:02:13.860
I might like the number 8.

52
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.399
To choose a random number, I'm going to go and say, OK,

53
00:02:17.399 --> 00:02:18.690
where's the list of responders?

54
00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:19.800
It starts at 15.

55
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.070
Actually, it starts at 16 because that's me.

56
00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.440
We're going to choose a random number between 16

57
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.210
and the end person 266.

58
00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.190
Oh, we just got-- oh.

59
00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.070
OK.

60
00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:33.570
OK.

61
00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.028
I'm going to cut it off right here.

62
00:02:35.028 --> 00:02:35.541
271.

63
00:02:35.541 --> 00:02:36.040
OK.

64
00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.490
16 and 271.

65
00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:39.330
Perfect.

66
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.380
OK.

67
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.880
I'm going to choose a random number.

68
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:42.900
I'm going to go to my IDE.

69
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.490
And you don't need to know how to do this yet,

70
00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.115
but by the end of this class, you will.

71
00:02:46.115 --> 00:02:49.191
I'm just going to use Python.

72
00:02:49.191 --> 00:02:51.690
I'm just going to get the random number package that's going

73
00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:52.950
to give me a random number.

74
00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.820
I'm going to say random.randint.

75
00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.510
And I'm going to choose a random number between 16 and 272,

76
00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.380
OK.

77
00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.440
75.

78
00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:03.690
OK.

79
00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:04.800
Great.

80
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:05.880
I chose a random number.

81
00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.557
And I'm going to find the number in the responder's sheet.

82
00:03:08.557 --> 00:03:09.640
What was the number again?

83
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.040
Sorry.

84
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.000
75.

85
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.020
OK.

86
00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.080
Up we go.

87
00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.710
There we go.

88
00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:21.300
Lauren Z-O-V. Yeah.

89
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.060
Nice.

90
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:22.710
You're here.

91
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.110
Awesome.

92
00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:37.810
All right.

93
00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:41.200
That's an example of me being a machine and also,

94
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.870
at the same time, using Python in my everyday life,

95
00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:48.650
just lecturing, to find a random number.

96
00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.350
Try to use Python wherever you can.

97
00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:51.725
And that just gives you practice.

98
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:55.770
That was fun.

99
00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.190
But we're at MIT.

100
00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:59.110
We're MIT students.

101
00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.900
And we love numbers here at MIT.

102
00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.749
Here's a numerical example that shows

103
00:04:04.749 --> 00:04:06.790
the difference between declarative and imperative

104
00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:10.000
knowledge.

105
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.470
An example of declarative knowledge

106
00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.530
is the square root of a number x is y such that y times y

107
00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.020
is equal to x.

108
00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:19.010
That's just a statement of fact It's true.

109
00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.872
Computers don't know what to do with that.

110
00:04:25.872 --> 00:04:27.830
They don't know what to do with that statement.

111
00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:31.270
But computers do know how to follow a recipe.

112
00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.250
Here's a well-known algorithm.

113
00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:36.550
To find the square root of a number x,

114
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:43.070
let's say x is originally 16, if a computer follows

115
00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.640
this algorithm, it's going to start with a guess, g,

116
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.620
let's say, 3.

117
00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.940
We're trying to find the square root of 16.

118
00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.320
We're going to calculate g times g is 9.

119
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.120
And we're going to ask is if g times g

120
00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:59.670
is close enough to x, then stop and say, g is the answer.

121
00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.699
I'm not really happy with 9 being really close to 16.

122
00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:04.490
So I'm going to say, I'm not stopping here.

123
00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:07.130
I'm going to keep going.

124
00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:08.690
If it's not close enough, then I'm

125
00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:12.920
going to make a new guess by averaging g and x over g.

126
00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.480
That's x over g here.

127
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.150
And that's the average over there.

128
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.120
And the new average is going to be my new guess.

129
00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.430
And that's what it says.

130
00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:27.680
And then, the last step is using the new guess,

131
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:28.790
repeat the process.

132
00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.610
Then we go back to the beginning and repeat the whole process

133
00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.484
over and over again.

134
00:05:33.484 --> 00:05:35.150
And that's what the rest of the rows do.

135
00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:36.590
And you keep doing this until you decide

136
00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:37.631
that you're close enough.

137
00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.140
What we saw for the imperative knowledge

138
00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:46.610
in the previous numerical example

139
00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:51.604
was the recipe for how to find the square root of x.

140
00:05:51.604 --> 00:05:53.270
What were the three parts of the recipe?

141
00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:56.090
One was a simple sequence of steps.

142
00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:59.270
There were four steps.

143
00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:02.660
The other was a flow of control, so there were

144
00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:05.360
parts where we made decisions.

145
00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:06.980
Are we close enough?

146
00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.320
There were parts where we repeated some steps.

147
00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.890
At the end, we said, repeat steps 1, 2, 3.

148
00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.320
That's the flow of control.

149
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:18.340
And the last part of the recipe was a way to stop.

150
00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.660
You don't want a program that keeps going and going.

151
00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:23.869
Or for a recipe, you don't want to keep baking bread forever.

152
00:06:23.869 --> 00:06:25.160
You want to stop at some point.

153
00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.800
Like 10 breads is enough, right?

154
00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:30.230
So you have to have a way of stopping.

155
00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.780
In the previous example, the way of stopping

156
00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.240
was that we decided we were close enough.

157
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:40.261
Close enough was maybe being within .01, .001,

158
00:06:40.261 --> 00:06:41.010
whatever you pick.

159
00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:46.550
This recipe is there for an algorithm.

160
00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:49.248
In computer science speak, it's going to be an algorithm.

161
00:06:49.248 --> 00:06:51.622
And that's what we're going to learn about in this class.

